1
00:00:00,803 --> 00:00:01,316
Hello.

2
00:00:01,316 --> 00:00:07,018
In the next few minutes, I'll introduce Microsoft Foundry with a demo we built ourselves.

3
00:00:07,018 --> 00:00:10,478
Contoso AI, which you'll see today, is the internal AI

4
00:00:10,478 --> 00:00:13,745
service of a fictional manufacturer called Contoso.

5
00:00:13,745 --> 00:00:18,807
Employees use it like ChatGPT, and administrators run it in the Foundry portal.

6
00:00:18,807 --> 00:00:22,523
Everything on screen is the result of a real run on Azure.

7
00:00:23,707 --> 00:00:26,190
Many companies succeed with an AI pilot.

8
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:30,599
But the moment they try to scale it across the company, they hit walls.

9
00:00:30,599 --> 00:00:34,758
First, you have to connect your own documents and systems securely.

10
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:39,725
Second, you have to keep choosing which model to use, among a flood of new ones.

11
00:00:39,725 --> 00:00:44,009
Third, you have to prove quality and security even after you go live.

12
00:00:44,009 --> 00:00:46,555
Foundry solves all three on one platform.

13
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:52,854
Microsoft Foundry is one platform where enterprises build and run AI apps and agents.

14
00:00:52,854 --> 00:00:55,739
At the top are your apps and business systems.

15
00:00:55,739 --> 00:00:59,127
Below them sit models and agents, tools and knowledge.

16
00:00:59,127 --> 00:01:03,769
Guardrails, evaluation and tracing live in the same project, and all of it

17
00:01:03,769 --> 00:01:08,285
rests on Entra ID, Azure's permission system, networking and monitoring.

18
00:01:08,285 --> 00:01:12,676
In other words, instead of collecting parts and bolting them together,

19
00:01:12,676 --> 00:01:15,938
you start from a platform that is already connected.

20
00:01:17,229 --> 00:01:18,633
Today has four parts.

21
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:21,975
First, we deploy everything with a single command.

22
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:25,451
Next, the app that employees use: agents that answer

23
00:01:25,451 --> 00:01:28,593
from documents, handle tasks, and analyze data.

24
00:01:28,593 --> 00:01:30,933
Then it's the administrator's turn.

25
00:01:30,933 --> 00:01:34,208
We'll see models, agents, guardrails, tracing and

26
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:37,952
evaluation in the real Foundry portal, exactly as it is.

27
00:01:37,952 --> 00:01:41,829
Finally, we wrap up with an enterprise adoption checklist.

28
00:01:43,783 --> 00:01:45,442
The architecture is simple.

29
00:01:45,442 --> 00:01:49,068
Employees use the Contoso AI app running on Container Apps,

30
00:01:49,068 --> 00:01:53,247
and the app reaches the Foundry project with Entra ID, with no keys.

31
00:01:53,247 --> 00:01:57,550
Inside the project are nine models, including GPT-6.1; agents that use

32
00:01:57,550 --> 00:02:02,036
tools such as document search and code execution; guardrails that protect

33
00:02:02,036 --> 00:02:06,093
inputs and outputs; and evaluation and tracing to measure quality.

34
00:02:06,093 --> 00:02:10,210
Administrators run the same project directly in the Foundry portal.

35
00:02:10,210 --> 00:02:13,591
And everything lives in a single region, Korea Central.

36
00:02:18,015 --> 00:02:20,425
Deployment is a single command: azd up.

37
00:02:20,425 --> 00:02:25,863
What you're about to see is a recording of a real run, with the waiting time compressed.

38
00:02:26,243 --> 00:02:27,946
First, the pre-flight checks.

39
00:02:27,946 --> 00:02:30,705
It verifies the required resource providers and

40
00:02:30,705 --> 00:02:33,347
generates the demo access code automatically.

41
00:02:38,953 --> 00:02:42,430
Next, a Bicep template creates the infrastructure:

42
00:02:42,430 --> 00:02:45,908
the Foundry resource and project, and nine models.

43
00:02:45,908 --> 00:02:49,733
That's GPT-6.1 Sol and Terra, Model Router, embeddings,

44
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:52,863
plus DeepSeek, Kimi, Llama, Mistral and Grok.

45
00:02:52,863 --> 00:02:57,105
The guardrail policy, the Application Insights connection and

46
00:02:57,105 --> 00:03:01,417
the least-privilege role assignments are in the same template.

47
00:03:09,907 --> 00:03:11,308
Then the container image.

48
00:03:11,308 --> 00:03:14,728
Even without Docker on your machine, Azure Container Registry

49
00:03:14,728 --> 00:03:18,259
builds it for you, and the image is deployed to Container Apps.

50
00:03:22,493 --> 00:03:26,356
Finally, when the app starts for the first time, it uploads eight

51
00:03:26,356 --> 00:03:30,220
policy PDFs to a vector store and creates four agents in Foundry.

52
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:32,597
That wait is handled automatically, too.

53
00:03:35,418 --> 00:03:36,658
And that's everything.

54
00:03:36,658 --> 00:03:39,363
The web address and the access code are printed.

55
00:03:39,363 --> 00:03:43,026
In production, you would replace this code with Entra ID sign-in.

56
00:03:53,749 --> 00:03:55,846
Now, the app that employees see.

57
00:03:55,846 --> 00:03:59,450
Enter the access code and a ChatGPT-style screen opens.

58
00:03:59,450 --> 00:04:02,399
On the left are agents for each kind of work,

59
00:04:02,399 --> 00:04:05,413
and your conversation history builds up below.

60
00:04:06,268 --> 00:04:08,045
Let's ask Contoso Guide first.

61
00:04:08,045 --> 00:04:11,184
I've been here four years, so how many days of annual

62
00:04:11,184 --> 00:04:14,620
leave do I get this year, and how do I request a half day?

63
00:04:14,972 --> 00:04:16,470
You can watch the agent work.

64
00:04:16,470 --> 00:04:20,395
It turns the question into search queries and finds relevant passages in the

65
00:04:20,395 --> 00:04:24,476
policy documents, and you can even see the document names and relevance scores.

66
00:04:25,227 --> 00:04:29,861
The answer is sixteen days, based on the HR-001 employment rules.

67
00:04:29,861 --> 00:04:32,855
Click a source and the original PDF opens.

68
00:04:32,855 --> 00:04:37,275
That one line is what reduces hallucinations and builds trust.

69
00:04:37,628 --> 00:04:39,126
Let's try a harder one.

70
00:04:39,126 --> 00:04:43,556
A team dinner for twelve people, 480,000 won, with alcohol included.

71
00:04:43,556 --> 00:04:46,292
Can it be reimbursed, and who approves it?

72
00:04:46,644 --> 00:04:51,594
It applies the meal limit and the alcohol rule together, and even shows the calculation.

73
00:04:51,594 --> 00:04:54,238
Whether this answer matches the policy exactly,

74
00:04:54,238 --> 00:04:57,444
we'll verify later with the evaluation in the admin view.

75
00:04:59,006 --> 00:05:02,516
This time, instead of an answer, let's hand over a task.

76
00:05:02,516 --> 00:05:05,462
We upload a team-dinner receipt to Contoso Pay.

77
00:05:08,122 --> 00:05:11,217
From the photo of the receipt, it reads the merchant,

78
00:05:11,217 --> 00:05:15,130
the items and the amounts, and even tells whether alcohol is on it.

79
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:20,386
It looks up the meal limit in the policy and calculates the claimable amount step by step.

80
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:30,626
Then it looks up the employee by ID and calls a function to submit the expense.

81
00:05:30,626 --> 00:05:31,661
This part matters.

82
00:05:31,661 --> 00:05:35,344
We configured submission to run only after a person approves it.

83
00:05:36,105 --> 00:05:39,058
The claimed amount and the personal share are laid out,

84
00:05:39,058 --> 00:05:41,958
and when you click approve, a ticket number is issued.

85
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:45,609
You hand the work to AI, but the responsibility stays with a person.

86
00:05:51,205 --> 00:05:52,607
Next is data analysis.

87
00:05:52,607 --> 00:05:56,114
We ask Contoso Analyst, which is connected to the sales

88
00:05:56,114 --> 00:05:59,365
and customer feedback files, to look for anomalies.

89
00:06:01,874 --> 00:06:05,483
The agent writes Python code itself and runs it in a sandbox.

90
00:06:05,483 --> 00:06:07,909
The calculation is not the model's guess.

91
00:06:07,909 --> 00:06:10,394
It is the result of actually running code.

92
00:06:19,157 --> 00:06:23,658
If we expand a finished step, the code the agent wrote appears as it is.

93
00:06:23,658 --> 00:06:26,909
You can verify exactly which calculations were done.

94
00:06:28,454 --> 00:06:29,501
Here is the result.

95
00:06:29,501 --> 00:06:33,413
It found that robot vacuum revenue in the Busan-Gyeongnam region turned

96
00:06:33,413 --> 00:06:37,050
down from July and returns went up, and it linked that to customer

97
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:41,072
feedback from the same period, most of which complains about the battery.

98
00:06:41,072 --> 00:06:43,166
The chart can be downloaded as a file.

99
00:06:47,552 --> 00:06:51,088
Questions about the latest technology go to Contoso Architect,

100
00:06:51,088 --> 00:06:54,110
which is connected to the Microsoft Learn MCP server.

101
00:06:54,110 --> 00:06:58,957
The basis is not the model's memory, but official documentation it has just searched.

102
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:02,264
Tools are added by connecting them, without changing code.

103
00:07:08,019 --> 00:07:10,763
In the tool call list, the sequence stays visible.

104
00:07:10,763 --> 00:07:14,659
It searches Microsoft Learn for documents, then looks for code samples.

105
00:07:14,659 --> 00:07:19,323
The commands in the answer are based on the official documentation it just retrieved.

106
00:07:20,675 --> 00:07:23,163
There is also a general chat with no agent.

107
00:07:23,163 --> 00:07:26,866
It is the closest to ChatGPT, and you pick the model at the top.

108
00:07:26,866 --> 00:07:30,164
Let's turn on web search and ask about the European Union

109
00:07:30,164 --> 00:07:33,635
battery regulation obligations a manufacturer needs to know.

110
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:42,538
It searched the latest web and answered with source links.

111
00:07:42,538 --> 00:07:47,268
You can also see which model produced the answer, how long it took, and the token count.

112
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:50,646
Now let's change only the model, on the same screen.

113
00:07:50,646 --> 00:07:52,683
Not OpenAI this time, but DeepSeek.

114
00:07:52,683 --> 00:07:55,301
We'll ask it to write an apology text message

115
00:07:55,301 --> 00:07:58,036
for customers who complained about the battery.

116
00:08:01,690 --> 00:08:04,936
The screen stays the same and only the model changed,

117
00:08:04,936 --> 00:08:08,794
because the call goes to the same endpoint of the same project.

118
00:08:09,149 --> 00:08:11,083
Finally, let's pick Model Router.

119
00:08:11,083 --> 00:08:14,717
It looks at how hard the question is, chooses a suitable model

120
00:08:14,717 --> 00:08:18,057
by itself, and shows below which model actually answered.

121
00:08:18,057 --> 00:08:21,749
The platform even takes the burden of choosing a model off you.

122
00:08:43,704 --> 00:08:44,590
Now, security.

123
00:08:44,590 --> 00:08:49,524
It happens all the time that an employee pastes customer information as it is.

124
00:08:49,524 --> 00:08:52,560
Let's turn on personal data masking and send it.

125
00:08:58,428 --> 00:09:02,476
It found the name, the ID number and the phone number and masked them,

126
00:09:02,476 --> 00:09:05,483
and only the masked sentence is passed to the model.

127
00:09:05,483 --> 00:09:09,588
The original text is not left in the chat history or the title, either.

128
00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:11,498
Next is a jailbreak attempt.

129
00:09:11,498 --> 00:09:16,309
Let's send the classic attack that tells the model to ignore its previous instructions.

130
00:09:31,493 --> 00:09:34,756
The Foundry guardrail blocked it before the model was even

131
00:09:34,756 --> 00:09:37,793
called, and it tells the employee which policy it hit.

132
00:09:37,793 --> 00:09:40,493
Administrators manage this policy in the portal.

133
00:09:42,316 --> 00:09:43,158
A quick pause.

134
00:09:43,158 --> 00:09:47,187
Let's look at how the agents we've seen so far were built, in code.

135
00:09:47,187 --> 00:09:50,554
This is the whole definition: the model deployment name,

136
00:09:50,554 --> 00:09:52,839
the instructions, and a list of tools.

137
00:09:52,839 --> 00:09:56,387
Add the document search tool and you get a knowledge agent.

138
00:09:56,387 --> 00:09:59,273
Add the code interpreter and you get an analyst.

139
00:09:59,273 --> 00:10:02,339
Add an MCP server address and you get an architect.

140
00:10:02,339 --> 00:10:06,368
Change the instructions or tools and recreate it, and a new version

141
00:10:06,368 --> 00:10:10,276
appears that you can compare with the previous one in the portal.

142
00:10:11,031 --> 00:10:12,670
Calling it is even simpler.

143
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:15,279
You connect with Entra ID instead of a key,

144
00:10:15,279 --> 00:10:18,252
and just tell the Responses API the agent's name.

145
00:10:18,252 --> 00:10:21,710
When you stream, the tool calls, sources and token counts

146
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:25,533
arrive as events, and our web screen simply draws those events.

147
00:10:25,533 --> 00:10:30,447
Now let's move on to the administrator's screen, where these agents are operated.

148
00:10:34,441 --> 00:10:36,824
Administrators start in the Foundry portal.

149
00:10:36,824 --> 00:10:38,875
The project we just deployed is open.

150
00:10:38,875 --> 00:10:43,698
You can see the project endpoint, and it shows that API key authentication is not used.

151
00:10:43,698 --> 00:10:47,689
Both apps and people come in only through Entra ID and role permissions.

152
00:10:51,726 --> 00:10:55,901
The catalog gathers models from many companies: OpenAI,

153
00:10:55,901 --> 00:10:59,622
Anthropic, Microsoft, Meta, Mistral and DeepSeek.

154
00:10:59,622 --> 00:11:03,342
Find the model you want and deploy it right away.

155
00:11:10,544 --> 00:11:12,726
This is the deployments screen.

156
00:11:12,726 --> 00:11:17,373
All nine models the app was using are here: GPT-6.1 Sol and Terra,

157
00:11:17,373 --> 00:11:21,034
Model Router, embeddings, and DeepSeek, Kimi, Llama,

158
00:11:21,034 --> 00:11:25,400
Mistral and Grok, managed in the same project in the same way.

159
00:11:31,505 --> 00:11:34,006
Open one deployment and you see its endpoint,

160
00:11:34,006 --> 00:11:36,951
its deployment type, and the tokens-per-minute limit.

161
00:11:36,951 --> 00:11:39,564
It shows that keys can't be used, and adding or

162
00:11:39,564 --> 00:11:42,065
swapping a model also happens on this screen.

163
00:11:43,339 --> 00:11:44,650
This is the agents screen.

164
00:11:44,650 --> 00:11:46,970
The four agents created from code are running,

165
00:11:46,970 --> 00:11:49,795
and the knowledge agent has already reached version two.

166
00:11:53,654 --> 00:11:58,328
Open one and you see the model, the instructions and the connected tools as they are.

167
00:11:58,328 --> 00:12:00,858
The knowledge agent has file search connected.

168
00:12:00,858 --> 00:12:03,607
From here you can edit the instructions, chat with

169
00:12:03,607 --> 00:12:06,302
it right away, and even save it as a new version.

170
00:12:06,655 --> 00:12:09,908
The same definition is also available as YAML, so you can put it

171
00:12:09,908 --> 00:12:13,111
in Git and run it through code review and deployment pipelines.

172
00:12:17,081 --> 00:12:21,491
In the details, you check the response endpoint and the publishing channels.

173
00:12:21,491 --> 00:12:25,031
You can publish to Teams, Microsoft 365 Copilot or a web app,

174
00:12:25,031 --> 00:12:28,745
which takes the agent into the tools your employees already use.

175
00:12:31,647 --> 00:12:33,726
This is the guardrails screen.

176
00:12:33,726 --> 00:12:37,329
The Contoso guardrail we deployed as code is applied

177
00:12:37,329 --> 00:12:40,863
to the two GPT-6.1 deployments and to Model Router.

178
00:12:41,216 --> 00:12:45,214
Jailbreak, indirect prompt injection and four content safety categories

179
00:12:45,214 --> 00:12:48,592
are all on, and you can expand any item to see its criteria.

180
00:12:48,592 --> 00:12:53,264
The request that was blocked in the app earlier was blocked by exactly this policy.

181
00:12:56,388 --> 00:12:57,109
Now, tracing.

182
00:12:57,109 --> 00:13:00,495
Every question an employee asked in the app is recorded here.

183
00:13:00,495 --> 00:13:03,436
You can see how long it took and the input and output

184
00:13:03,436 --> 00:13:06,156
tokens, and some have evaluation scores attached.

185
00:13:10,175 --> 00:13:13,673
Open one and you see how long each step took, from the app's

186
00:13:13,673 --> 00:13:17,521
request to the agent call, the document search and the model call.

187
00:13:17,521 --> 00:13:22,127
When an answer is slow or odd, you can find the step that caused it right away.

188
00:13:29,999 --> 00:13:34,329
Finally, the most important question: how do we know it really got better?

189
00:13:34,329 --> 00:13:38,074
This evaluation scored the version before we connected knowledge

190
00:13:38,074 --> 00:13:41,351
and the version after, on the same twenty-two questions.

191
00:13:41,717 --> 00:13:45,676
Version two, with knowledge connected, passed most of the items,

192
00:13:45,676 --> 00:13:48,583
while version one, without it, falls far short.

193
00:13:48,583 --> 00:13:53,346
You prove improvement with numbers, not with a feeling, and before deployment

194
00:13:53,346 --> 00:13:57,677
you can run the same evaluation to stop a version whose quality drops.

195
00:14:01,447 --> 00:14:05,739
For administrators responsible for operations, there is the Operate screen.

196
00:14:05,739 --> 00:14:09,402
You see every project and agent in the subscription at a glance,

197
00:14:09,402 --> 00:14:13,351
with the number of running agents, the success rate, cost and alerts.

198
00:14:24,403 --> 00:14:27,244
In Management, the Quota screen shows each deployment's

199
00:14:27,244 --> 00:14:30,757
tokens-per-minute limit and usage, and lets you request an increase.

200
00:14:30,757 --> 00:14:34,219
It's the screen that keeps responses from suddenly getting blocked.

201
00:14:39,747 --> 00:14:43,011
In the project details you manage roles per user, and under

202
00:14:43,011 --> 00:14:46,220
connected resources you confirm that Application Insights,

203
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:49,762
which collects the traces, is already connected to this project.

204
00:14:49,762 --> 00:14:52,417
Employees use the app, administrators operate in

205
00:14:52,417 --> 00:14:54,963
the portal, and both look at the same project.

206
00:14:56,700 --> 00:15:00,889
Here are the questions that come up first in enterprise adoption.

207
00:15:00,889 --> 00:15:02,307
First, access control.

208
00:15:02,307 --> 00:15:07,593
Entra ID and Azure role-based access control separate what people and apps can do,

209
00:15:07,593 --> 00:15:11,847
and even this demo connects with a managed identity, with no keys.

210
00:15:11,847 --> 00:15:13,071
Second, networking.

211
00:15:13,071 --> 00:15:17,648
Private endpoints and virtual networks can close off internet exposure.

212
00:15:17,648 --> 00:15:18,421
Third, data.

213
00:15:18,421 --> 00:15:21,450
Customer prompts and data are not used to train

214
00:15:21,450 --> 00:15:24,415
the base models, and stored data is encrypted.

215
00:15:24,415 --> 00:15:26,478
Fourth, where data is processed.

216
00:15:26,478 --> 00:15:31,699
You choose the resource region and the deployment type to meet your requirements.

217
00:15:31,699 --> 00:15:33,697
Fifth, security and governance.

218
00:15:33,697 --> 00:15:38,660
Defender, Purview and Azure Policy carry threat detection and policy forward.

219
00:15:38,660 --> 00:15:39,433
Sixth, cost.

220
00:15:39,433 --> 00:15:42,592
You cap each deployment with a quota, and reserve

221
00:15:42,592 --> 00:15:46,201
provisioned throughput when you need stable performance.

222
00:15:46,201 --> 00:15:50,971
We configured this demo with a public endpoint for demonstration purposes.

223
00:15:50,971 --> 00:15:54,516
In production, turning these items on is the next step.

224
00:15:56,367 --> 00:15:57,052
To sum up.

225
00:15:57,052 --> 00:16:02,323
First, a single command deploys models, agents, guardrails and observability.

226
00:16:02,323 --> 00:16:08,211
Second, employees get answers from documents and get work done in a ChatGPT-style app.

227
00:16:08,211 --> 00:16:12,114
Third, administrators operate models, agents, guardrails,

228
00:16:12,114 --> 00:16:15,948
tracing and evaluation in one place, the Foundry portal.

229
00:16:15,948 --> 00:16:20,535
Fourth, security and quality are proven with code and with numbers.

230
00:16:21,408 --> 00:16:22,973
The next step is simple.

231
00:16:22,973 --> 00:16:26,364
Pick one highly repetitive task, connect the related

232
00:16:26,364 --> 00:16:29,560
documents, and write twenty evaluation questions.

233
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:33,668
Deploy everything you saw today yourself, with this repository.

234
00:16:33,668 --> 00:16:34,320
Thank you.
